---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Contact | braininjurycalc.com';
const description = 'Contact the editorial team at braininjurycalc.com. For brain injury cases, please consult a licensed catastrophic injury attorney with neurotrauma experience.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Contact</h1>

  <p>Editorial questions, citation suggestions, corrections, and interview requests are all welcome at <a href="mailto:hey@theclicklab.agency">hey@theclicklab.agency</a>. We read every email and reply within a few business days.</p>

  <h2>What we can help with</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Pointing you to the relevant guide or section of the site for a specific question.</li>
    <li>Correcting errors. If you spot a misstatement, an outdated citation, a broken link, or a calculator output that does not match the documented methodology, please tell us with the page URL and what is wrong.</li>
    <li>Editorial inquiries from reporters, attorneys, paralegals, and researchers.</li>
    <li>Citation requests. If you would like to cite this site in a paper, brief, or article, the editorial bylines and methodology page are stable starting points.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What we cannot help with</h2>

  <p>We are not a law firm. We do not represent clients, review individual cases, give legal advice, file documents, or appear in court. The Click Lab is an independent editorial operation; the contributors are credentialed paralegals with substantive neurotrauma experience, not licensed attorneys. Nothing on this site, and no email exchange with us, creates an attorney-client relationship.</p>

  <p>We are not medical providers. We cannot interpret imaging, advise on treatment, or substitute for the neurology, rehabilitation, and neuropsychology specialists who manage TBI care.</p>

  <h2>If you have an actual brain injury case</h2>

  <p>The single most important step is consultation with a licensed catastrophic injury attorney in your state who has substantive neurotrauma experience. Brain injury cases are technically complex &mdash; they involve neuroimaging interpretation, neuropsychological evaluation, life-care planning, vocational impact analysis, and often multi-defendant liability theories. General personal injury practitioners can handle smaller TBI cases competently, but moderate-to-severe TBI cases benefit substantially from a firm that handles catastrophic injury as a primary practice.</p>

  <p>Most catastrophic injury attorneys offer free initial consultations and work on contingency (no fee unless they recover). State and local bar associations maintain referral services; the Brain Injury Association of America (BIAA) and many state-level brain injury associations also maintain attorney resources.</p>

  <h2>Privacy when you email us</h2>

  <p>Email you send us is received by our email provider and retained for as long as needed to respond. We do not share email contents with third parties, do not sell email addresses, and do not add inbound senders to any marketing list. See our <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> for full detail.</p>

  <h2>Mailing address</h2>

  <p>The Click Lab is an online editorial operation without a public mailing address. All correspondence should go through <a href="mailto:hey@theclicklab.agency">hey@theclicklab.agency</a>.</p>
</BaseLayout>
